Title: "Writing Aliases"
Id: "f2e9ef30-5ad5-42c8-b50a-72f9a31f33a9"

Text: "A writing alias is `<form-or-declaration> :~> <raw latex>`. The body is not
parsed as formulation syntax; it is whatever trimmed, non-empty text follows the
first top-level `:~>`.

```mlg-fragment
alpha :~> \alpha
beta :~> \beta
f(x_) :~> f? \left( x_? \right)
```

In a `Writing:` section — the collection-wide block, or a per-item override —
each alias must be written as a **double-quoted** string and its left side must
be a plain `Name`:

```mlg-fragment
Writing:
. \"alpha :~> \alpha\"
. \"beta :~> \beta\"
```

The quotes are required precisely because the raw LaTeX body could otherwise be
misread as a formulation.

Writing aliases are distinct from the nested `Documented:` `writing:`/`as:`
item, which renders a mapping introduced by `Declares:` rather than a name."
Id: "f719bf43-607d-4118-bf1a-b9396bb862c7"
